//! Mapping: one installed protection row, with a handle on it (spec §2.5).
//!
//! A Mapping is an installed grant row. It records which process's domain
//! carries the row and which row it is; `base()` reads the address that row was
//! placed at. On a tier that does not translate, that address is the region's
//! own.
//!
//! Only a Mapping unmaps (§2.5), which is the object's reason to exist. That is
//! why `Process.map` answers with one instead of a status: without a handle
//! onto the row there would be no way to take it back.
//!
//! It names only the floor, so `sos.system`, which produces one, can name it.

import sosabi.{SosStatus, MappingHandle, MappingOp, NO_HANDLE, MINT_OP}

import sos.floor.{call_value, checked, sos_handle_release, sos_mapping_unmap,
                  ALL_RIGHTS}

/// A handle to a Mapping, one protection row, installed.
///
/// Built by `Process.map`, and by `System.stats_region_map` inside the
/// `StatsRegion` it answers.
///
/// `NoCopy`, and dropping it releases the handle, which is not an unmap. The
/// RAII habit points the other way, so read this carefully. Release destroys
/// the caller's entry and never the row, so a `Mapping` that falls out of
/// scope leaves its row installed: the mapping is permanent, safe but leaked,
/// never a dangling-unmap hazard. A caller that means to revoke calls `unmap()`
/// and then drops the value.
///
/// Don't unmap in a `deinit`: that would make the row's lifetime the wrapper's,
/// and a wrapper drops on every path out of a scope, including a launcher's
/// right after it hands a child its memory (map into a child, then start it).
/// The mapping would be revoked before the child ever ran.
///
/// A Mapping owns its row; the row is not a counted reference back. So a
/// Mapping with every handle released and the grant still installed frees its
/// slot and leaves the row, which is then unremovable until the target
/// process's teardown.
public struct Mapping {
    public(package) handle: MappingHandle,
}

extension Mapping: NoCopy {
    /// Drop is release, and release is not unmap; see the type's docstring.
    /// Otherwise this is `System`'s deinit exactly: the sentinel and the
    /// discard.
    func deinit(&var self) {
        if self.handle != NO_HANDLE {
            let _ = sos_handle_release(self.handle)
        }
    }
}

extension Mapping {
    /// Take the row out of the domain it was installed in. Requires
    /// `MappingRight.Unmap`.
    ///
    /// The memory becomes unreachable to the target process at once, not at its
    /// next switch, even when the target is the process running right now. An
    /// `unmap` that returned `Ok` and left the window open until some later
    /// reschedule would be a revocation that did not revoke.
    ///
    /// Unmapping twice terminates the caller (`BadState`). Unmapping a Mapping
    /// whose target process has died answers `Err(SosStatus.Revoked)`: its
    /// whole domain went with it, so no row is left to remove, and the holder
    /// could not have checked.
    ///
    /// It consumes nothing. The wrapper stays valid and still owns its handle,
    /// so "unmap, then drop" is one ordinary sequence; no word moves between
    /// tables, only a row leaving a record.
    public func unmap(&self) -> Result<Void, SosStatus> {
        checked(sos_mapping_unmap(self.handle))
    }

    /// Where this mapping is. Requires `MappingRight.Base`.
    ///
    /// A caller supplies no address to `map` on any tier (a hint is an opinion
    /// no tier could honor uniformly), so the kernel chooses, and a program
    /// treats what comes back as data. This is the portable way to learn the
    /// address; a build-time constant is right only while user addresses are
    /// physical ones.
    ///
    /// The number is a user address in the target's address space: the caller's
    /// own when a process maps into itself, the child's when a launcher maps
    /// into a child. Two processes holding two Mappings of one region may get
    /// two different answers; on a tier that does not translate they get the
    /// same one. A portable program assumes neither.
    ///
    /// Asking an already-unmapped Mapping ends the caller with `BadState`, and
    /// one whose target has died answers `Err(SosStatus.Revoked)`, the same two
    /// refusals as `unmap`.
    public func base(&self) -> Result<UInt, SosStatus> {
        call_value(self.handle, MappingOp.Base as UInt, 0, 0, 0)
    }

    /// Mint a sibling of this Mapping handle, narrowed by a keep mask: the
    /// universal `MINT_OP`, on `System.mint`'s exact terms. Requires the
    /// universal `MappingRight.Mint` on this handle.
    ///
    /// `rights` is what the sibling may keep: its rights are this handle's
    /// intersected with the mask, so the default (all ones) is "the same
    /// rights", and naming a right this handle does not hold is a no-op.
    /// The mask is where a holder writes policy for an installed
    /// row: a sibling that keeps `Base` and not `Unmap` can report
    /// where the row is and cannot take it out.
    ///
    /// `Mint` is itself maskable. The sibling is independent (its own slot,
    /// generation and release) and names the same object. A caller at its
    /// handle quota gets `SosStatus.QuotaExceeded` and a full handle table is
    /// `SosStatus.NoResource`; a handle without `Mint` terminates the caller.
    public func mint(&self,
                     rights: UInt32 = ALL_RIGHTS) -> Result<Mapping, SosStatus> {
        let raw = try call_value(self.handle, MINT_OP, rights as UInt, 0, 0)
        return Mapping(handle: MappingHandle(raw))
    }
}
